{"title":"What I’ve been reading","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/913844939/0/marginalrevolution~What-Ive-been-reading.html","date":1740636311000,"content":"<p>Alain Mabanckou, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Dealing-Dead-Alain-Mabanckou/dp/1800817703/ref=sr_1_1?crid=8AHZF2K5FK43&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.t90tpqDFoG6Ym8ZC8svA-Tyc1m7Qx-KBd1uIt7A17RSwhklyhzR5mLf5uFo8bRnWJUtZkduRTq1Vcbvj9zgXSHymw5m18B7ZR63ZLM-aKWgMNI_W-MAL-aLlMw94R78SSOEzG7bzmk9QeAPOnEUVjCLH2gu6trtkigVEssbI569JpY9trcJmeHXfztsBI4G6OEy05h_2aBYqTuvBjl4ay-Opw3XYLmpYbuMe1ZLGgFo.2HBgE_8J8q5ElRWjapz_IFxJyATZA-LEq_UVHUO8UqU&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=Dealing+with+the+Dead&amp;qid=1740611764&amp;sprefix=dealing+with+the+dead%2Caps%2C68&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">Dealing with the Dead</a>.  Most African fiction does not connect with me, and there is a tendency for the reviews to be untrustworthy.  This “cemetery memoir,” from the Congo (via UCLA), connected with me and held my interest throughout.</p>\r\n<p>Philip Zaleski and Carol Zaleski, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Fellowship-Literary-Inklings-Barfield-Williams/dp/0374536252/ref=sr_1_1?crid=3E1SRTBGVUJVB&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.yCA9-GquZk3zyWEV0t7P0g.UhKdlnydff5S-L3uZykKUWqXK7fq9H0J0eqMXjKJAds&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=The+Fellowship%3A+The+Literary+Lives+of+the+Inklings%3A+J.R.R.+Tolkien%2C+C.S.+Lewis%2C+Owen+Barfield%2C+Charles+Williams&amp;qid=1740611891&amp;sprefix=the+fellowship+the+literary+lives+of+the+inklings+j.r.r.+tolkien%2C+c.s.+lewis%2C+owen+barfield%2C+charles+williams%2Caps%2C129&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">The Fellowship: The Literary Lives of the Inklings: J.R.R. Tolkien, C.S. Lewis, Owen Barfield, Charles Williams</a>.  I was in the mood of thinking I don’t need to read another book about these people.  Yet this one was so good it won me over nonetheless.</p>\r\n<p>Eddie Huffman, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Doc-Watson-Music-American-Roots/dp/1469680351/ref=sr_1_1?crid=3R6HG5F26F5NK&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.VBCN912LF7e3vxokqTEqax1g4UZVDFuZbHbwn7z9gcyrXgR0WkYvJDBwscBzDYckG84fj7vayiunuk9PVD4zAPbJgpCLOtGjrkn5BScDZrbLK7URO-dFRfAKSdSzZsgQ8EouRPXXKYcJvqRwi3n8aqmyD1w0_eAc6uq_XnjydlKMpZjXMkLKSrUwnq3X3WIKRXbu1ED6uFv2JIpoYSppN79qo2hqRJcByiejyEsdPB0.LNnorqvSkTLg-4RH_TrOf_yWp7Gin11iEpscz-3A2Wo&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=doc+watson+a+life+in+music&amp;qid=1740611931&amp;sprefix=doc+watson+a+life%2Caps%2C73&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">Doc Watson: A Life in Music</a>.  A fun book about one of America’s <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6VAbrnjdtYw\" target=\"_blank\">greatest guitarists</a>.  Watson was blind from an early age, and he was collecting state disability benefits until he was 40 — a classic late bloomer.</p>\r\n<p>Philip Freeman, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Brewing-Luminous-Music-Cecil-Taylor/dp/3955932613/ref=sr_1_1?crid=1WLEHPUOJVOR0&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.o3Gqi2eM5JRdYcA4oJkx_g.6d5T_WE3KPkKIAJ3Bm4ipd5Fd67KOU6DLCr2OCmCVbM&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=In+the+Brewing+Luminous%3A+The+Life+and+Music+of+Cecil+Taylor&amp;qid=1740612069&amp;sprefix=in+the+brewing+luminous+the+life+and+music+of+cecil+taylor%2Caps%2C103&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">In the Brewing Luminous: The Life and Music of Cecil Taylor</a>.  Call me crazy, but I think Sun Ra and Taylor are better and more important musically than say Duke Ellington.  Freeman’s book is the first full-length biography of Taylor, and it is well-informed and properly appreciative.  It induced me to buy another book by him.  The evening I saw Taylor was one of the greatest of my life, I thank my mother for coming with me.</p>\r\n<p>Carlos M.N. Eire, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/They-Flew-Impossible-Carlos-Eire/dp/0300259808/ref=sr_1_1?crid=X21GZWU9LAS6&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9._HoKRs7LfZROiOfRpz5vGw.iJqv1dUIL3anIfuADe406ZUdJEfz3RO37uHhWsLBK50&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=They+Flew%3A+A+History+of+the+Impossible&amp;qid=1740612111&amp;sprefix=they+flew+a+history+of+the+impossible%2Caps%2C76&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">They Flew: A History of the Impossible</a>.  Ross Douthat recommended this one to me.  It is well done, and worth reading, but I don’t find it shifted my priors on whether “impossible” events might have really happened.</p>\r\n<p>I agree with the central arguments of Samir Varma’s <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Science-Free-Will-Determinism-Everything/dp/1803417315\" target=\"_blank\">The Science of Free Will: How Determinism Affects Everything from the Future of AI to Traffic to God to Bees</a>.  I was happy to write a foreword for the book.</p>\r\n<p>Kathleen deLaski, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Who-Needs-College-Anymore-Imagining/dp/1682539520\" target=\"_blank\">Who Needs College Anymore? Imagining a Future Where Degrees Don’t Matter</a>.  One of a growing chorus of books suggesting higher education is on the verge of some radical changes.</p>\r\n<p>There is Daniel Brook, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Einstein-Sex-Magnus-Hirschfeld-Visionary/dp/1324007249/ref=sr_1_1?crid=2V6JQM88CJEKM&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.QLLohB_pvDrmg-I1BuKKiw.GSkFkH9zjKx0L1rWGgqW6H97cOZKEl6Dhf0lKlbZVh8&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=The+Einstein+of+Sex%3A+Dr.+Magnus+Hirschfeld+Visionary+of+Weimar+Berlin&amp;qid=1740614730&amp;s=books&amp;sprefix=the+einstein+of+sex+dr.+magnus+hirschfeld+visionary+of+weimar+berlin%2Cstripbooks%2C151&amp;sr=1-1\" target=\"_blank\">The Einstein of Sex: Dr. Magnus Hirschfeld Visionary of Weimar Berlin</a>.  It is good to see him getting more attention.</p>\r\n<p>There is also Brandy Schillace, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Intermediaries-Weimar-Story-Brandy-Schillace/dp/1324036311/ref=sr_1_1?crid=1N1DZMAD4XLWU&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.WfRiWc0Pq6ctppwhAR3pFg.fbfCoNlyZCJ7sqrJtUbDesg6dEtlB8kxnUBURH6qCto&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=The+Intermediaries%3A+A+Weimar+Story&amp;qid=1740614765&amp;s=books&amp;sprefix=the+intermediaries+a+weimar+story%2Cstripbooks%2C63&amp;sr=1-1\" target=\"_blank\">The Intermediaries: A Weimar Story</a>.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/02/what-ive-been-reading-266.html\">What I’ve been reading</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/913844939/0/marginalrevolution\" />\r\n<div><a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/28/913844939/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/fblike20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/29/913844939/marginalrevolution,\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/pinterest20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/24/913844939/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/x.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/19/913844939/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/email20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/20/913844939/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/rss20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/02/what-ive-been-reading-266.html#comments\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/comments20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/02/what-ive-